  "requirement": "The `habeas-requirements` skill MUST direct an agent that cannot ask the user in the session to stop, leaving the drafts uncommitted, and report the revised drafts and the design questions.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The requirement has one obligation. The `habeas-requirements` skill must direct an agent that cannot ask the user to do three things: stop, leave the drafts uncommitted, and report the drafts with the design questions.\n\nThe test `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_requirements_skill_stops_and_reports_when_it_cannot_ask_the_user]` is marked `{test}` for the subject. It calls `assert_phrases(\"habeas-requirements\", ...)`. That helper reads the bundled skill through `bundled_skill`, which joins `SKILL.md` and its reference files and collapses whitespace. It then runs `assert_text`, which asserts that the text contains the phrase. The phrase is \"If you cannot ask the user in this session, stop, leave the drafts uncommitted, and report them with the design questions\".\n\nThe phrase covers the condition (cannot ask the user) and all three directives (stop, leave the drafts uncommitted, report the drafts with the design questions). The search for \"cannot ask the user\" showed the same wording in `assets/skills/habeas-requirements/SKILL.md`, in the Workflow section. There it is hard-wrapped across lines, so the whitespace collapse is what lets the phrase match.\n\nThe requirement governs static skill text, so asserting the directive's presence is the right check. The skill has no other mode or boundary that the requirement distinguishes. The test would fail if any part of the directive were removed or reworded, and I found no gap.",
